READ_ME for Mustard Seed and Card Builder

OVERVIEW - THE BIG PICTURE

The Mobile Mustard Seed (index.html) is a web-based flashcard/question-answer application built with Spring Boot, hosted on Render with an 
Aiven MySQL database. The app is a Maven project built in the Eclipse IDE and primarily uses pure Thymeleaf templates to display
in html format the Strings stored in ArrayLists in the Json database type format. It works in tandem with the Card Builder (cardmaker.html) which 
is used to create, retrieve, update and delete questions and answers stored as QuestionAnswer objects in ArrayLists which are in 
turn stored as Json text objects in a MySql Database. 

The Mustard Seed flashcard test taker is available in mobile and 
desktop formats. The concept is simple. A user selects a topic which displays a question, and selecting 'Answer' displays the
correct answer. 'Next' brings up the next question. All questions can be displayed sequentially, the default, or randomly. Selecting a 
different topic clears the previous fields and begins anew. The subject matter of topics can be virtually anything. Answers can be 
simple (2 + 2 = 4) or may contain detailed explanations. This particular version tests one's knowledge of scripture verses and fundamental
theological and apologetic concepts, mostly in a true/false format. Currently only subjects Verses[a] and Verses[b], which are simple flipcards,
are populated. In addition the topics Atheism and Trinity tests one's knowledge at a deeper level, in true/false format. Note, that
no record is kept of correct or incorrect answers. Like regular flashcards, repetition is the key. 

The Mustard Seed card builder (cardmaker.html) is similarly straightforward and not overly complex. When landing on cardmaker.html no subject 
is selected. Selecting a subject from the far-left buttons column loads that particular subject, its name being displayed at the top of 
the Active Subject Workspace. If any questions/answers populate a given subject they will automatically be displayed below 
in a Live Collection Deck Table. If no questions/answers exist yet for a particular subject simply type a question in the second 'Question' text area, 
and enter the answer in the third 'Answer' text area. Click 'Add the Card' to add that Question/Answer object to an ArrayList which will be
inserted into the relevant database table; it will also appear in the below Collection Deck Table. To update or delete a Question/Answer simply
find the ID in the Collection Deck Table, type that number in the first ID text area, click 'Find a Card' to bring it up, make your changes and select 
'Update the Card'. Or, after finding the desired card select 'Delete the Card' to remove it from the subject list.


THE MYSQL DATABASE - Inserting a list of QuestionAnswer objects

When a user clicks 'Add the Card' to populate a subject ArrayList<QuestionAnswer> with a QuestionAnswer object the 
CardController.java's @PostMapping("/subject/{id}")'s method is invoked which creates the
QuestionAnswer object which is passed to the addCard() method of the service (CardServiceImpl.java)
which in turn gets (or creates) a List<QuestionAnswer> cardList from the database, inserts the card into
this list and returns the list to the database by invoking the service's saveToDatabase() method. 

Each row in the MySql database table 'flashcards' has three columns, a) id  (bigint), b) questionsandanswers (json arraylist 
'object'), and c) studysubject (varchar). At its initial inception ten rows have been created, each with a value of null. This
preferred technique fixes the primary key for each row without shuffling indexes when inserting, deleting, or updating, and
provides much needed consistency. The list for topic 'Atheism' will always reside in the second row, id '2'. 
 
After a QuestionAnswer object is created in the CardController.java it is added to the list by calling cardService.addCard(id, newItem);
In this addCard() service method a List<QuestionAnswer> cardList is either created (or retrieved from the database). The new QuestionAnswer
is added to the list and saved to the database using the service's saveToDatabase(subjectId, subjectName, cardList) method. And it is inside 
this saveToDatabase() method that the java list is mapped to a Json string for insertion into the 
db table using Jackson's ObjectMapper: String jsonString = objectMapper.writeValueAsString(list); 
 
Finding, updating and deleting QuestionAnswer objects essentially uses the same process to retrieve or set the 
questionsandanswers column of the 'flashcards' table. Study the endpoints and methods in the CardController.java
class to track the mapping, etc. and the back-and-forth flow of the list of QuestionAnswer objects, and the identifying 
studysubject column Strings. 

MOBILE MUSTARD SEED - Taking the test

When the user (test-taker) first lands on the Mustard Seed landing page, index.html, the end point @GetMapping("/")
public String showPage(Model model, HttpSession session) method of FlipController.java first clears out stale HttpSession
parameters (ie., session.removeAttribute("currentQuestionsList"). Then it transfers String data from this controller to
front-end UI template Thymeleaf, information displayed in the browswer interface such as 
model.addAttribute("myTextValue", "Please select a topic to begin."). The default value of various flags are also set here.

When the user selects a topic from the drop-down combo-box menu in index.html, the FlipController 
fires @PostMapping("/select-topic") public String selectTopic(@RequestParam("topic") String topic, 
HttpSession session, Model model). This method retrieves a List<QuestionAnswer> questions from the database
as explained above. This Java list is set in the session, session.setAttribute("currentQuestionsList", questions), 
along with other attributes, and persists "until the user closes the bowser, logs out, or the session times out." (Google)

And it is here, in selectTopic() that the drop-down is activated, the first question is retrieved from the session
list and set in the model as an attribute to be returned via Thymeleaf to the Question textarea of index.html.

The controller's PostMapping for getting the answer is @PostMapping("/fetch-answer")
public String getAnswerForCurrentQuestion(HttpSession session, Model model). Here, depending on whether
the active deck is shuffled, the answer to the retrieved from that deck list and added to 
the model as an attribute to be displayed in the GUI: 
	QuestionAnswer currentCard = activeDeck.get(currentIndex);
	model.addAttribute("myTextValue", currentCard.getQuestion());
	model.addAttribute("myTextAnswer", currentCard.getAnswer());
	
Clicking 'Next Question' follows the same logic and is self-explanatory. Although it is
immportant to understand how the index order is calculated: 
currentIndex = (currentIndex + 1) % activeDeck.size(); 

Lastly is the option to cycle through a sequential list or randomize it. The logic for this
process is found in the FlipController.java's @PostMapping("/toggle-shuffle")
	public String toggleShuffle(HttpSession session, Model model) endpoint. 
Selecting 'Switch to Random' returns the current list from the session: 
	List<QuestionAnswer> baseList = (List<QuestionAnswer>) session.getAttribute("currentQuestionsList");
The baseList is cloned, then shuffled, and then set as a session attribute:
List<QuestionAnswer> randomizedDeck = new ArrayList<>(baseList);
Collections.shuffle(randomizedDeck);
session.setAttribute("shuffledQuestionsList", randomizedDeck);
activeWorkingList = randomizedDeck;	

